<div class="lab" data-controller="encoder">
  <header class="lab__head">
    <h1>Avro Lab</h1>
    <p class="lab__lede">
      Write a JSON object, pick a repeat count, then download it: as plain JSON
      (<strong>baseline</strong>), as an un-compressed Avro container, or as an Avro container
      with <strong>deflate</strong> (zlib) block compression — the browser decodes both back to JSON.
      Each run draws a timeline bar — schema derivation (server, Avro only), encode (server),
      download (client), decode (client) — and logs the full numbers to the console. A checkbox
      shows or hides the schema segment (on by default). Transfer size and latency are visible in the
      browser's network tab.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="lab__panel">
    <label class="lab__label" for="json_text">JSON source</label>
    <textarea id="json_text" class="lab__editor" data-encoder-target="jsonText" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" rows="14"><%= default_sample_json %></textarea>
  </div>

  <div class="lab__panel">
    <label class="lab__label" for="repeats">Repeat size</label>
    <div class="lab__knob">
      <input id="repeats" type="range" min="1" max="100000" step="1" value="100" list="repeat-ticks" data-encoder-target="repeats" data-action="input->encoder#syncRepeats">
      <output class="lab__knob-value" data-encoder-target="repeatOutput">100</output>
    </div>
    <datalist id="repeat-ticks">
      <option value="1"></option>
      <option value="1000"></option>
      <option value="10000"></option>
      <option value="50000"></option>
      <option value="100000"></option>
    </datalist>
  </div>

  <div class="lab__panel lab__actions">
    <button type="button" class="lab__button" data-action="encoder#encodeJson" data-encoder-target="jsonButton">1 · Download as JSON (baseline)</button>
    <button type="button" class="lab__button lab__button--avro" data-action="encoder#encodeAvroNaive" data-encoder-target="naiveButton">2 · Download as Avro · naive (Ruby interpreter overhead)</button>
    <button type="button" class="lab__button lab__button--avro" data-action="encoder#encodeAvro" data-encoder-target="avroButton">3 · Download as Avro (null codec)</button>
    <button type="button" class="lab__button lab__button--avro" data-action="encoder#encodeAvroDeflate" data-encoder-target="avroDeflateButton">4 · Download as Avro (deflate)</button>
    <button type="button" class="lab__button lab__button--go" data-action="encoder#encodeGo" data-encoder-target="goButton">5 · Download as Avro (Go endpoint · deflate)</button>
  </div>

  <div class="lab__panel lab__options">
    <label class="lab__checkbox" for="show_schema">
      <input id="show_schema" type="checkbox" checked data-encoder-target="showSchema" data-action="change->encoder#toggleSchema">
      Show Avro schema derivation segment
    </label>
  </div>

  <div class="lab__panel lab__status" data-encoder-target="status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>

  <div class="lab__panel lab__note">
    <p><strong>Who wins here?</strong> This lab pays the <strong>Ruby interpreter price</strong> on the
    encode bar — the JSON arm is a single C call (<code>json</code> gem); the Avro arm is a pure-Ruby
    gem encoding field-by-field. Avro earns its cost where it's amortized: <strong>write once, read
    many</strong> (Kafka-style pipelines), where compactness and schema evolution matter — not
    per-request CPU on a web server.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="lab__panel lab__note lab__note--callout">
    <p><strong>CPU-bound on Avro?</strong> Encode in a compiled language instead — <strong>Java or
    Go</strong> — where the same payload encodes orders of magnitude faster than the pure-Ruby gem.</p>
  </div>
</div>